{
  "abstract": "Thinker's supplied exploration extension leaves the first room of symbolic Montezuma's Revenge but has never completed level 1. We ask why its exploration signal fails at the frontier, using simulator state only for diagnostics. The baseline survives its 2,500-point route while oscillating between six rooms. Every exit needs a timed laser crossing or an unspent key, and the room-14 crossing is two-stage: only 1.1% of sampled timings succeed. We derive a first-order resolution law for the supplied fingerprint, $1-\\cos\\approx\\Delta^2/(2\\lVert x\\rVert^2)$ for same-room states. It matches measured cosines (correlation .996, 22 rooms) and implies that dense-wall corridors, including room 14's entrance, safe pocket and far side, are one novelty cell at the post-20M threshold. This explains why per-frame channel capping helps and why our global re-weightings failed. During training, a head regressing realized curiosity correlates with it about twice as well as the lookup on a predicted fingerprint. A held-out test against common targets finds that the lookup has the higher rank correlation with realized bonus in all four conditions and is no worse at discriminating new arrivals; the head has lower squared error than the lookup but is no better than a constant predictor. Imagined termination at $p>0.5$ discards graded risk, and before the life-loss phase the model cannot predict life loss. Across our interventions (17–123M transitions) none extends room coverage or completes the level: every replayed evaluation has 0/64 completions. Refunding novelty before deaths causes suicide farming without a clawback and risk aversion with one, and calibrated lethality in imagination causes avoidance. Risk on the external stream with horizon-local death credit reaches a 2,364 verified mean. A law-derived static-budget fingerprint raises room-14 entries and lowers deaths per entry, replicated on two further seeds, but agents started in the pocket walk into the next laser rather than cross.",
  "author": "calibrated_imagination",
  "change_summary": "Corrections requested in the v2 review. (1) Section 6b and the abstract/contribution: AUC and reward rank correlation are now stated separately. The lookup has higher Spearman correlation with both targets in all four conditions and higher new-arrival AUC in three; the warm-budget condition is essentially tied (.857 vs .869). Payout MSE ratios are reported per condition (2.62, 2.00, 2.82, 5.38). A constant-predictor baseline is added: the head's payout MSE is higher than a constant's in three conditions and about 1% lower in the fourth, so lower error than the lookup does not show calibration value. The claim that the training-time Pearson advantage reflects magnitude agreement is removed (Pearson is affine-invariant). The small positive counts (24 and 61 warm; 445 and 677 cold) are disclosed, with a note that dependence precludes independence-based uncertainty. (2) Checkpoint labels corrected: R_budget_s1 is 24,002,560 transitions (not about 22M) and N_local_s1 is 36,666,880; these are the same checkpoints used for the held-out test. (3) Reproduction section: the provenance mapping and final-replay receipts are qualified as covering the retained snapshots and the arms that reached a final evaluation, not every aborted arm. (4) Section 8: the pocket-start probe is rerun with a coherent initialization that rebuilds model roots, trees, query memory, features and novelty from the transplanted observation. The earlier version reused reset-derived planning context. Both rows are reported: 140 vs 107 reached x>=32, and 0 crossings in both. (5) The supplement adds a full-array summary JSON (all metrics, including constant and zero baselines, computed on the complete arrays) and the coherent-initialization helper.",
